		<title>Security researchers show how cheap AI inference providers steal keys and snoop on users</title>
		<link>https://the-decoder.com/security-researchers-show-how-cheap-ai-inference-providers-steal-keys-and-snoop-on-users/</link>
					<comments>https://the-decoder.com/security-researchers-show-how-cheap-ai-inference-providers-steal-keys-and-snoop-on-users/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Matthias Bastian]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:07:56 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI in practice]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Cybersecurity]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://the-decoder.com/?p=43182</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="1376" height="768" src="https://the-decoder.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cybersecurity_data_leak.png" class="attachment-full size-full wp-post-image" alt="" style="height: auto; margin-bottom: 10px;" decoding="async" /></p>
<p>        Security researchers at Dragos used two million fake AI requests to uncover serious vulnerabilities in the AI supply chain, finding at least three compromised providers, hundreds of stolen AWS keys, and marketplace resellers who were simply reading customer requests. OpenRouter responded by removing three providers and 125 endpoints.</p>
<p>The article <a href="https://the-decoder.com/security-researchers-show-how-cheap-ai-inference-providers-steal-keys-and-snoop-on-users/">Security researchers show how cheap AI inference providers steal keys and snoop on users</a> appeared first on <a href="https://the-decoder.com">The Decoder</a>.</p>
